= Application-specific integrated circuit =

== Overview ==
An '''application-specific integrated circuit''' ('''ASIC''') is an integrated circuit designed to implement a specified application or family of functions, rather than provide a general-purpose, field-reconfigurable fabric. The term covers digital standard-cell designs, mixed-signal and custom circuits, and structured ASIC variants. ASICs are not necessarily single-function or single-customer chips.

== Design and implementation ==
A digital standard-cell ASIC typically proceeds through requirements and architecture, register-transfer-level description, functional verification, logic synthesis using a technology library, floorplanning, placement, clock-tree synthesis, routing, timing and physical verification, fabrication, packaging, and production test. The precise flow varies with technology, circuit type and foundry. A process design kit (PDK) and characterized cells connect circuit implementation to fabrication constraints.

== Design styles ==
* '''Standard-cell ASIC:''' logic assembled from characterized library cells, with physical placement and interconnect optimized for a target process.
* '''Full-custom ASIC:''' transistor and layout choices tailored more directly to the function, commonly used for analog, memory or performance-critical blocks.
* '''Structured ASIC:''' partially predefined fabric or layers customized for an application; cost and flexibility depend on platform.

== Economics and limitations ==
Design, verification, tooling, masks and qualification incur non-recurring engineering (NRE) costs. Manufacturing cost per usable die depends on wafer cost, area, yield, testing and packaging. Lower per-unit cost or better power/performance/area is possible at sufficient volume, but is not guaranteed. Revisions after tapeout may require costly redesign and new masks.

== Distinction from programmable logic ==
An [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuit]] can be custom-manufactured or field-programmable. An ASIC's fabricated functionality is generally not reconfigured like an FPGA fabric, although its embedded firmware, processors and configuration registers may be programmable. [[Computer architecture]] and [[Semiconductor]] provide related context.

